Under proportional and near-proportional effects, $\lambda(C)$ remains within $[0,1]$ for ${\geq}99\%$ of configurations with 100\% sign preservation---the Theorem~\ref{thm:monotone} conditions are satisfied by real LLM confusion matrices. Even under non-proportional effects ($f$ independent of $g$), sign preservation remains at 97.3\%, with $\lambda(C)$ exceeding $[0,1]$ in 15\% of cases. The per-$K$ breakdown shows that $\lambda(C)$ is dimension-dependent: $K{=}2$ (mean 0.249), $K{=}3$ (mean 0.082), $K{=}10$ (mean 0.490), with 100\% in $[0,1]$ across all $K$ under proportional effects. Sparse DGP (zero-effect categories) produces the highest violation rate (24.2\%), consistent with the breakdown mechanism: zero-effect categories make $f/g$ ratios undefined, maximally departing from proportionality.

\subsection{Case Study: ASV Diagnosis of Political Ad Tone Classification}
\label{sec:asv_case_study}

We illustrate the ASV diagnostic workflow on a concrete CSS application. \citet{egami_hinck_stewart_wei2023} use LLM classifiers to annotate political advertising tone ($K{=}3$: promote/contrast/attack) from the Fowler \& Ridout database, then estimate the causal effect of ad tone on voter outcomes. The annotated variable is the \textbf{exposure} (treatment). Using a representative $K{=}3$ confusion matrix from our empirical set (VAST DeepSeek zero-shot, mean diagonal 0.734) as a proxy:

\begin{center}
\small
\begin{tabular}{lccc}
\toprule
Coefficient & $\delta^*_{\text{mag}}$ (10\%) & $\delta^*_{\text{sf}}$ & Relative bias at $\delta{=}1$ \\
\midrule
$\beta_1$ (primary) & 0.234 & $> 1.5$ & 39.7\% \\
$\beta_2$ (secondary) & 0.154 & $> 1.5$ & 60.4\% \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

Both coefficients are magnitude-unsafe ($\delta^* < 1$), with the secondary coefficient more fragile ($\delta^*{=}0.154$). If the same annotator were instead classifying a \textbf{confounder} (e.g., media framing as a control variable), magnitude ASV rises to $\delta^*{=}0.690$---a $3{\times}$ increase in error tolerance from the same confusion matrix. This demonstrates the central insight: topology, not annotator quality, determines vulnerability. Algorithm~\ref{alg:correction} routes this exposure topology to PPI++ or DSL correction; matrix-based methods (naive plug-in, MLA) are ineffective due to FWL non-applicability.

\subsection{End-to-End Demonstration}
\label{sec:e2e_demo}

\input{e2e_demo_table.tex}

The demonstration uses a symmetric $C_0$ ($d_{\mathrm{rel}}{=}0$); empirical $K{=}3$ matrices deviate substantially (median $d_{\mathrm{rel}}{=}0.33$, Remark~\ref{rem:symmetry}), but core ASV ranking and correction routing remain valid under Conjecture~\ref{conj:monotone}.

\subsection{Semi-Real Case Study with Production LLM Confusion Matrices}
\label{sec:real_cm_demo}

We repeat the end-to-end pipeline using 10 confusion matrices estimated from production LLM annotation of VAST stance detection ($K{=}3$; DeepSeek, Llama4, Qwen2.5-7B, Qwen3 under zero-shot and few-shot prompting). These matrices exhibit median $d_{\mathrm{rel}}{=}0.33$ (Remark~\ref{rem:symmetry}), far from the symmetric assumption of Theorem~\ref{thm:k3_symmetric}. Results confirm both topology-dependent patterns: PPI++ achieves 93.6\% median exposure bias reduction while plug-in corrections yield 0\% improvement for confounding (FWL invariance, machine precision; Table~\ref{tab:e2e-real-cm}).

\input{e2e_real_cm_table.tex}

\subsection{Correction Method Sensitivity to $K$}
\label{sec:correction_k_sensitivity}

We test whether the correction method ranking from Table~\ref{tab:correction} ($K{=}3$) is stable at $K{=}5$ and $K{=}10$. For each $K$, we sample 5 random Dirichlet confusion matrices (diagonal $\geq 0.6$), run 100 MC replications per topology--matrix combination ($N{=}5{,}000$, 10\% gold fraction), and compute RMSE reduction for all five methods across all seven topologies.

\paragraph{Grand ranking stability.}
Spearman rank correlations across all topology--method pairs: $\rho_{3,5} = 0.80$, $\rho_{3,10} = 0.84$, $\rho_{5,10} = 0.93$ (all $p < 10^{-8}$). Per-topology correlations are even stronger: 5 of 7 topologies (confounding, collider, M-bias, IV, and exposure at $K{=}10$) achieve $\rho = 1.0$ between $K{=}3$ and $K{=}10$.

\paragraph{Method-specific patterns.}
PPI++ effectiveness degrades with $K$: confounding RMSE reduction drops from 85.6\% ($K{=}3$) to 75.6\% ($K{=}10$); mediation drops from 57.1\% to 9.0\%. At $K{=}10$, PPI++ becomes harmful for IV ($-124\%$) and exposure ($-11\%$). MC-SIMEX is the most $K$-robust method, maintaining the top rank for 5/7 topologies at $K{=}10$ (vs.\ 3/7 at $K{=}3$). FWL invariance (naive plug-in and MLA achieving 0\% bias reduction on the treatment coefficient) holds at all $K$ tested, confirming the structural nature of this result.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\small
\caption{Top correction method and RMSE reduction (\%) by topology across $K$. Bold = top method for that topology--$K$ combination.}
\label{tab:correction_k}
\begin{tabular}{lccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Topology} & $K{=}3$ & $K{=}5$ & $K{=}10$ \\
\midrule
Confounding & \textbf{PPI++} 85.6 & \textbf{PPI++} 76.4 & \textbf{PPI++} 75.6 \\
Mediation & \textbf{PPI++} 57.1 & \textbf{SIMEX} 59.7 & \textbf{SIMEX} 59.0 \\
Collider & \textbf{SIMEX} 55.4 & \textbf{SIMEX} 63.4 & \textbf{SIMEX} 57.3 \\
Exposure & \textbf{SIMEX} 72.5 & \textbf{SIMEX} 65.0 & \textbf{SIMEX} 47.3 \\
M-bias & \textbf{SIMEX} 3.2 & \textbf{SIMEX} 47.0 & \textbf{SIMEX} 43.7 \\
IV & \textbf{SIMEX} 30.9 & \textbf{SIMEX} 30.4 & \textbf{SIMEX} 27.1 \\
Front-door & \textbf{PPI++} 82.9 & \textbf{PPI++} 69.0 & \textbf{SIMEX} 70.1 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{Comparison with Related Sensitivity Frameworks}
\label{sec:sensitivity_comparison}

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{Comparison of sensitivity frameworks.}
\label{tab:sensitivity_comparison}
\small
\begin{tabular}{lccc}
\toprule
& \textbf{ASV} & \textbf{E-value} & \textbf{Partial} $R^2$ \\
\midrule
Parameter space & $K{\times}K$ confusion $C(\delta)$ & Scalar confounder & $R^2$ of omitted var \\
Sensitivity path & $(1{-}\delta)I + \delta C_0$ & Monotone scalar & Monotone scalar \\
Topology-specific & \checkmark & \ding{55} & \ding{55} \\
Sign-flip diagnostic & \checkmark ($\delta^*_{\text{sign}}$) & \ding{55} & \ding{55} \\
Topology dependence & \checkmark (7 topologies ranked) & \ding{55} & \ding{55} \\
Monotonicity & Proved $K{=}2$; conj.\ $K{\geq}3$ & Guaranteed & Guaranteed \\
Target & Annotation error & Unmeasured confounding & Omitted variable \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{Per-Topology $F_1$--Bias Correlation}
\label{sec:within_topo_f1}

The cross-topology correlation between macro $F_1$ and $|\text{bias}|$ is weak ($\rho = -0.10$, $p = 0.45$; \S\ref{sec:empirical}), but decomposing by topology reveals a structured pattern. Table~\ref{tab:within_topo_f1} reports Spearman correlations computed over 23 LLM configurations (HumAID $K{=}10$, VAST $K{=}3$, CivilComments $K{=}2$; 161 total scenarios).

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\small
\caption{Within-topology Spearman $\rho(F_1, |\text{bias}|)$ across 23 empirical LLM configurations $\times$ 3 datasets. Negative $\rho$: higher $F_1$ $\to$ lower bias (expected). Positive $\rho$: higher $F_1$ $\to$ higher bias (protective attenuation removed).}
\label{tab:within_topo_f1}
\begin{tabular}{lccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Topology} & $n$ & $\rho$ & $p$ \\
\midrule
Confounding & 23 & $-0.557$ & 0.006 \\
Mediation & 23 & $-0.565$ & 0.005 \\
Exposure & 23 & $-0.531$ & 0.009 \\
Front-door & 23 & $-0.568$ & 0.005 \\
IV & 23 & $-0.700$ & $<$0.001 \\
Collider & 23 & $+0.310$ & 0.150 \\
M-bias & 23 & $+0.867$ & $<$0.001 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Five topologies show the expected negative correlation: better annotation quality ($F_1{\uparrow}$) reduces causal bias. IV shows the strongest effect ($\rho = -0.70$), consistent with the Wald estimator's sensitivity to misclassification in the first stage. M-bias uniquely reverses: $\rho = +0.87$ ($p < 0.001$). The mechanism is that misclassification attenuates the spurious M-bias path, so the naive estimator under noisy annotations is \emph{closer} to the true causal effect; improving annotation quality (higher $F_1$) removes this protective attenuation and \emph{increases} bias. This is consistent with the correction results (\S\ref{sec:correction}), where PPI++ harms M-bias ($-$88\%) by removing the same protective noise. Collider shows a positive but non-significant trend ($\rho = +0.31$, $p = 0.15$), consistent with Berkson bias attenuation. Per-dataset breakdowns (CivilComments $K{=}2$: $\rho = -1.0$ for all topologies with $n{=}7$) should be interpreted with caution given the small sample and limited $F_1$ variation within the binary task.

\subsection{Confusion Matrix Estimation Robustness}
\label{sec:c_robustness}

The ASV framework requires the confusion matrix $C$, which in practice must be estimated from a held-out gold-labeled subset. We assess how estimation noise in $\hat{C}$ propagates to the safe/fragile partition and ASV ranking. For each of 8 empirical confusion matrices (VAST, 4 LLMs $\times$ 2 prompting strategies), we draw $n_{\text{gold}} \in \{50, 100, 200, 500\}$ samples from a $\text{Multinomial}(n_{\text{gold}}, C_{\cdot k})$ distribution per column, compute $\hat{C}$, and evaluate the resulting ASV ranking across 200 bootstrap replicates.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\small
\caption{$\hat{C}$ estimation robustness: safe/fragile partition stability and ASV ranking correlation (Spearman $\rho$ vs.\ true-$C$ ranking) under multinomial bootstrap from finite gold sets. 8 empirical matrices $\times$ 200 bootstrap replicates per $n_{\text{gold}}$.}
\label{tab:c_robustness}
\begin{tabular}{lccc}
\toprule
$n_{\text{gold}}$ & Partition stability & $\rho$ (median) & $\rho$ ($q_{25}$) \\
\midrule
50 & 50.4\% & 0.991 & 0.789 \\
100 & 82.0\% & 1.000 & 0.991 \\
200 & 85.6\% & 1.000 & 0.991 \\
500 & 88.5\% & 1.000 & 0.991 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

The ASV ranking is highly robust: median Spearman $\rho \geq 0.99$ even at $n_{\text{gold}}{=}50$. The safe/fragile partition (5 safe vs.\ 2 fragile topologies) recovers reliably from $n_{\text{gold}} \geq 100$ (82\% stability), increasing to 89\% at $n_{\text{gold}}{=}500$. The remaining instability concentrates in source matrices with low diagonal dominance (e.g., VAST DeepSeek few-shot: $\min C_{kk} = 0.16$), where bootstrap noise can shift borderline ASV values across the partition boundary. All 8 truth partitions match the canonical safe/fragile partition, confirming that the theoretical dichotomy holds empirically.

\subsection{Sign Reversal Determinants}
\label{sec:sign_determinants}

We investigate which confusion matrix features predict exposure sign reversal across the 23 empirical LLM configurations. For each matrix, we compute 10 structural features: $\min_k C_{kk}$ (minimum diagonal), $\overline{C_{kk}}$ (mean diagonal), $\max_{j \neq k} C_{jk}$ (maximum off-diagonal), $\max_{j \neq k} C_{jk}/C_{kk}$ (off-diagonal ratio), $\log(\kappa(C))$ (log condition number), mean and max column entropy, spectral gap, $\det(C)$, and minimum singular value. Sign reversal is defined as any exposure coefficient reversing sign under misclassification.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\small
\caption{Sign reversal rates by $K$ and best single-feature decision rule. Rule: $\det(C) < 0.014 \to$ sign reversal (accuracy 91.3\%, sensitivity 85.7\%, specificity 100\%). An AND rule ($\min_k C_{kk} < 0.55$ and mean entropy $> 0.40$) achieves 95.7\% accuracy with 100\% sensitivity.}
\label{tab:sign_determinants}
\begin{tabular}{lcc}
\toprule
$K$ & $n$ & Sign reversal rate \\
\midrule
2 & 7 & 0/7 (0\%) \\
3 & 8 & 7/8 (87.5\%) \\
10 & 8 & 7/8 (87.5\%) \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Binary matrices ($K{=}2$) never produce exposure sign reversal, consistent with classical attenuation theory. For $K \geq 3$, the determinant of $C$ is the strongest single predictor: $\det(C) < 0.014$ identifies sign-reversing matrices with 100\% specificity (zero false positives) and 85.7\% sensitivity. An AND rule combining $\min_k C_{kk} < 0.55$ with mean column entropy $> 0.40$ achieves 100\% sensitivity at 88.9\% specificity. These rules are robust to $\beta$-scaling (accuracy unchanged at $0.3{\times}$ and $3{\times}$ effect magnitude) but sensitive to $\beta$-sign structure: under uniform-positive $\beta$ (all coefficients same sign), no matrices produce sign reversal regardless of $C$, as cancellation across differently-signed coefficients is the underlying mechanism.

\subsection{Y-Stratified Non-Differential Verification}
\label{sec:y_stratified_test}

We directly test the non-differential assumption (Definition~\ref{def:nondiff}) by stratifying confusion matrices by outcome-proxy variables and testing $C(Y{=}\text{high}) \approx C(Y{=}\text{low})$. Using 12 LLM configurations on VAST ($K{=}3$; 4 models $\times$ 3 prompting strategies, 1{,}500 samples each), we compute per-stratum confusion matrices under three Y-proxies and apply permutation tests ($B{=}10{,}000$, test statistic: $\max_{j,k} |C^{(0)}_{jk} - C^{(1)}_{jk}|$).

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\small
\caption{Y-stratified non-differential test. Content-independent Y (text length) shows no significant violation (1/12 rejection $\approx$ expected false positive rate at $\alpha{=}0.05$). Content-dependent proxies (topic, sentiment) show expected violations since LLMs observe text content.}
\label{tab:y_stratified}
\begin{tabular}{lccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Y proxy} & \textbf{Type} & \textbf{Reject ($\alpha{=}0.05$)} & \textbf{Median $p$} \\
\midrule
Text length & Content-indep. & 1/12 & 0.490 \\
Topic & Content-dep. & 11/12 & 0.002 \\
Sentiment & Content-dep. & 10/12 & 0.017 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

The single text-length rejection (Llama-4 few-shot-3, $p{=}0.027$) is consistent with the expected false positive rate ($0.05 \times 12 = 0.6$). Content-dependent proxies (topic, sentiment) show systematic rejection because LLMs process text content from which these variables can be inferred, violating $Y \perp \text{text} \mid A$. In causal inference applications, $Y$ is typically an external outcome (e.g., health status, economic indicator) that cannot be inferred from the annotated text, satisfying the independence condition. The content-dependent rejections confirm statistical power: the test can detect violations when they exist.

\section{Notation}
\label{app:notation_table}

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\small
\caption{Key notation used throughout the paper.}
\label{tab:notation}
\begin{tabular}{cl}
\toprule
\textbf{Symbol} & \textbf{Description} \\
\midrule
$K$ & Number of annotation categories \\
$C$ & Column-stochastic confusion matrix, $C_{jk} = P(A^*{=}j \mid A{=}k)$ \\
$A$, $A^*$ & True and observed (misclassified) annotation \\
$T$ & Treatment variable \\
$Y$ & Outcome variable \\
$\tau$ & Causal effect of interest \\
$\delta$ & ASV perturbation parameter, $C(\delta) = (1{-}\delta)I + \delta C_0$ \\
$\delta^*$ & Critical ASV value (magnitude/significance/sign-flip) \\
$B(C)$ & Bias function, $= \operatorname{plim}(\hat{\tau}) - \tau$ \\
$\beta_k$ & Treatment effect coefficient for category $k$ \\
$D_k$ & Dummy variable for category $k$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\section{Use of AI Assistants}
\label{app:ai-assistants}

We used Claude (Anthropic) as an AI coding and writing assistant throughout this work. Specifically:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Code:} drafting experiment scripts, data-processing pipelines, and analysis utilities; debugging and refactoring.
    \item \textbf{Writing:} drafting and revising paper sections, polishing prose, and reformatting \LaTeX{}.
    \item \textbf{Proofs:} assisting with proof development for Theorems~1--2 and the conditional monotonicity analysis (Remark~\ref{rem:conditional_monotonicity}); all proofs were verified by the authors.
    \item \textbf{Literature:} summarizing related papers to inform the related-work section.
\end{itemize}

